1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:07,950
This video shows you how to find the original line behind a record that already arrived.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:19,188
In Elasticsearch, a record says risk high, from 10.2.7.44 to port 22, at 05:47:27.

3
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:26,510
firewall-parse removes _raw, so Elasticsearch never got the original line.

4
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:31,470
But the source, Kafka, still keeps it, until its retention runs out.

5
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:34,008
Capture from firewall again.

6
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:43,030
Keep only records that mention 10.2.7.44.

7
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:50,670
A capture from a source reads each record as it arrived, so the filter tests _raw.

8
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:55,263
Under Advanced, start at a time just before the record.

9
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,233
Start it, and use these records.

10
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:14,870
Find the record you saw in Elasticsearch: its time is 05:47:27.

11
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:18,479
Its output matches Elasticsearch.

12
00:01:18,479 --> 00:01:22,670
Risk high, from 10.2.7.44 to port 22.

13
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:25,672
And here is its original line.

14
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:30,944
These records ran through the pipeline as it is now.

15
00:01:30,944 --> 00:01:35,190
If it changed since the record went through, the output can differ.

16
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:37,107
Check on learning.

17
00:01:37,307 --> 00:01:41,797
Why can this capture reach a record from before it started?

18
00:01:46,197 --> 00:01:50,944
Kafka keeps records, so a capture can start at an earlier time.

19
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:54,709
A source that keeps nothing can't go back.

20
00:01:55,609 --> 00:02:01,169
Why does the capture's filter test _raw, not src_ip?

21
00:02:05,569 --> 00:02:12,425
A capture from a source reads records as they arrived, before the pipeline made src_ip.

22
00:02:13,325 --> 00:02:17,480
Next, find out why a record did not arrive.
